summary:
On 3 February 2026, a single frost caused losses of up to 90% of potato production in five municipalities of the Bolivian Altiplano (La Paz and Oruro), according to CIPCA field technicians. Now NOAA forecasts a strong El Niño for the 2026–27 summer. Using 45 years of daily NASA POWER data, we found that in 4 of the 5 strong El Niño events since 1981, rainfall in Obrajes (Oruro, 3,809 m) was below normal, by up to 40%.

Raíz helps a farmer and their technician compare three real crop rotations (potato, quinoa, faba bean, fallow) in 9 cases, with the El Niño year falling on each crop. For each case it shows:

the well-water irrigation that would have been needed;
frost days during flowering;
days of bare soil under heavy rain or wind.

Raíz does not tell farmers what to plant. It shows how their options performed in similar past years. The same result reaches farmers without internet as a printable sheet and a 45-second radio-ready bulletin in Spanish and Quechua, recorded by a local farmer.
